{"format":"kards-original-reference","version":1,"id":"8aaaa2455f91c6e5","name":"card_event_regia_marina","package":"/Game/Blueprints/Cards/Italy/events/card_event_regia_marina","parent":"UBaseCardObject","card":{"title":"REGIA MARINA","text":"Draw the highest cost card in your deck. Repeat once if it did not start there.","flavorText":"The Regia Marina was the Royal Italian Navy, serving the Kingdom of Italy from 1861 until 1946. It entered WWII as a large and modern force on paper, with battleships, cruisers, destroyers, torpedo boats, submarines, and many fast attack craft. Its main task was to control the central Mediterranean, protect supply routes to North Africa, and challenge British naval forces based in Gibraltar, Malta, and Alexandria. Italian warships were often fast and well-armed, but the fleet faced serious limits. It lacked a true fleet air arm, had few radar-equipped ships, and suffered from fuel shortages. These problems hindered its performance in the night battles and convoy actions that dominated the Mediterranean campaign. Heavy losses at Taranto and Cape Matapan showed the danger of British air power, radar and intelligence. Nevertheless, the Regia Marina still achieved some important successes. Italian frogmen badly damaged HMS Queen Elizabeth and HMS Valiant at Alexandria in 1941, while surface forces and aircraft helped disrupt convoys around Malta in 1942. Its submarines also fought in the Atlantic, and Italian small craft served as far away as the Black Sea and Lake Ladoga, in Karelia.","faction":"Italy","type":"order","rarity":"Rare","cost":2,"operationCost":0,"attack":0,"defense":0},"defaults":{"UberGraphFrame":{},"highestCost":-1,"Name":"card_event_regia_marina","title":{"Namespace":"card_italy","Key":"card_event_regia_marina_title","SourceString":"REGIA MARINA","LocalizedString":"REGIA MARINA"},"Type":"ETypeEnum::order","faction":"EFactionEnum::Italy","kredits":2,"Text":{"Namespace":"card_italy","Key":"card_event_regia_marina_text","SourceString":"Draw the highest cost card in your deck. Repeat once if it did not start there.","LocalizedString":"Draw the highest cost card in your deck. Repeat once if it did not start there."},"flavorText":{"Namespace":"card_italy","Key":"card_event_regia_marina_history","SourceString":"The Regia Marina was the Royal Italian Navy, serving the Kingdom of Italy from 1861 until 1946. It entered WWII as a large and modern force on paper, with battleships, cruisers, destroyers, torpedo boats, submarines, and many fast attack craft. Its main task was to control the central Mediterranean, protect supply routes to North Africa, and challenge British naval forces based in Gibraltar, Malta, and Alexandria. Italian warships were often fast and well-armed, but the fleet faced serious limits. It lacked a true fleet air arm, had few radar-equipped ships, and suffered from fuel shortages. These problems hindered its performance in the night battles and convoy actions that dominated the Mediterranean campaign. Heavy losses at Taranto and Cape Matapan showed the danger of British air power, radar and intelligence. Nevertheless, the Regia Marina still achieved some important successes. Italian frogmen badly damaged HMS Queen Elizabeth and HMS Valiant at Alexandria in 1941, while surface forces and aircraft helped disrupt convoys around Malta in 1942. Its submarines also fought in the Atlantic, and Italian small craft served as far away as the Black Sea and Lake Ladoga, in Karelia.","LocalizedString":"The Regia Marina was the Royal Italian Navy, serving the Kingdom of Italy from 1861 until 1946. It entered WWII as a large and modern force on paper, with battleships, cruisers, destroyers, torpedo boats, submarines, and many fast attack craft. Its main task was to control the central Mediterranean, protect supply routes to North Africa, and challenge British naval forces based in Gibraltar, Malta, and Alexandria. Italian warships were often fast and well-armed, but the fleet faced serious limits. It lacked a true fleet air arm, had few radar-equipped ships, and suffered from fuel shortages. These problems hindered its performance in the night battles and convoy actions that dominated the Mediterranean campaign. Heavy losses at Taranto and Cape Matapan showed the danger of British air power, radar and intelligence. Nevertheless, the Regia Marina still achieved some important successes. Italian frogmen badly damaged HMS Queen Elizabeth and HMS Valiant at Alexandria in 1941, while surface forces and aircraft helped disrupt convoys around Malta in 1942. Its submarines also fought in the Atlantic, and Italian small craft served as far away as the Black Sea and Lake Ladoga, in Karelia."},"rarity":"ERarityEnum::Rare","cardSet":"ECardSetEnum::OceaniaStorm","soundEnterPlay":{"ObjectName":"SoundWave'War_UI_Drum_Movement_Field_Snares_3'","ObjectPath":"kards/Content/War_UI/Waves/Drum_Movements/War_UI_Drum_Movement_Field_Snares_3.0"},"historyPhoto":{"AssetPathName":"/Game/Assets/Textures/HistoricalPhotos/REGIA_MARINA.REGIA_MARINA","SubPathString":""},"historyPhotoDescription":{"Namespace":"card_italy","Key":"card_event_regia_marina_photo","SourceString":"Italian battleships Littorio and Vittorio Veneto in the Mediterranean. Ca. 1940-43.","LocalizedString":"Italian battleships Littorio and Vittorio Veneto in the Mediterranean. Ca. 1940-43."},"OnAttackVisualsSoft":{"AssetPathName":"/Game/Blueprints/Effects/BP_AttackOrder.BP_AttackOrder_C","SubPathString":""},"Notes":"Image needed to be customized","isDefenseBuff":true},"variables":[{"name":"myDeck","type":"TArray<int32>","direction":"in"},{"name":"highestCost","type":"int32","direction":"in"},{"name":"cardToDraw","type":"int32","direction":"in"},{"name":"possible_cards","type":"TArray<UBaseCardObject*>","direction":"in"},{"name":"isFirstIteration","type":"bool","direction":"in"}],"functions":[{"name":"ExecuteUbergraph_card_event_regia_marina","owner":"card_event_regia_marina_C","params":[{"name":"EntryPoint",